Yes. For finals, your plan should be **not “add all tricks,” but build a controlled high-ceiling finals version**.

## Finals priority list

### 1. Keep Pantheon as frozen fallback

Do not delete or mutate the current qualifier bot.

[
\boxed{\text{Pantheon-stable = emergency fallback}}
]

Everything else should be in separate files.

---

### 2. Build one finals candidate: `Pantheon-F`

Goal:

[
\text{increase scoop rate without becoming a punt bot}
]

Your current bot is good for qualification, but capped because:

[
\text{fold too much, call too little, scoop too little}
]

Finals bot needs more continuation and better stack conversion.

---

### 3. Fix overfolding to aggression, but only slightly

Main issue:

[
\text{tight open range} + \text{high fold-to-pressure}
]

For finals, add a **small raise-range discount**, not a global loosen-up.

Recommended scale:

[
\text{villain raise strength} \times= 0.98 \text{ or } 0.99
]

Only apply it to:

* flop / turn,
* small or medium raises,
* loose or high-action opponents,
* heads-up or simple pots.

Do **not** apply it to:

* river big raises,
* all-ins,
* multiway heavy action,
* passive/nit opponents suddenly raising.

---

### 4. Add river danger discipline

Your bust hands suggest a small real leak:

[
\text{strong one-pair / overpair} \rightarrow \text{villain keeps betting} \rightarrow \text{dangerous river} \rightarrow \text{payoff}
]

Add a specific guard for:

* completed flush boards,
* four-straight boards,
* paired boards after heavy action,
* river raise after villain already showed strength.

This should not make you fold sets or premiums blindly. It should mainly protect:

[
\text{top pair, overpair, second pair, weak kicker top pair}
]

---

### 5. Add big-stack pot-control mode

This is one of the best finals ideas.

Trigger:

[
\text{hero stack} \geq 1.5 \times \text{average live stack}
]

Then:

* reduce thin value raises,
* call more with medium-strength hands,
* avoid reopening action with non-nut made hands,
* still bet and raise very strong hands.

This protects table lead without killing value.

---

### 6. Improve calling, not just raising

Top bots are not just more aggressive. They call more.

Your current call rate is too low. Finals version should move from:

[
\text{raise/fold}
]

toward:

[
\text{raise/call/fold with better thresholds}
]

Add calls mainly in:

* medium-equity hands versus small bets,
* bluff-catcher spots versus aggressive opponents,
* hands with showdown value where raising is too thin.

Do not widen garbage preflop.

---

### 7. Anti-station value mode

If weak bots become bad copycats, they will call too much.

Against high-call, low-raise opponents:

* value bet larger,
* do not bluff too much,
* do not over-respect calls,
* respect rare raises.

Rule:

[
\text{high call + low raise} = \text{station, value hard, fold to big raise}
]

---

### 8. Anti-maniac mode

Against high-raise or jam-heavy opponents:

* call down slightly wider with strong bluff-catchers,
* stop folding every medium-strength hand,
* but keep all-in discipline.

Rule:

[
\text{high raise + high all-in} = \text{reduce fold-to-pressure, but do not stack off weak top pair}
]

---

### 9. Optional sizing jitter

For finals only, test small deterministic sizing jitter.

Use only if A/B is neutral or positive.

Purpose:

[
\text{reduce hardcoded exploitability}
]

But do not ship if it changes EV negatively. Sizing changes are not cosmetic.

---

### 10. Do not re-enable board narrowing unless it wins A/B

You already found board narrowing hurt because many bots are board-illiterate.

So default:

[
\boxed{\text{keep board narrowing disabled}}
]

Only reconsider if finals opponents are demonstrably more board-aware.

---

## Testing order

Do not test everything at once. Use this order:

[
\text{Pantheon}
\rightarrow +\text{tiny raise-range discount}
\rightarrow +\text{river danger discipline}
\rightarrow +\text{big-stack mode}
\rightarrow +\text{anti-station / anti-maniac reads}
\rightarrow +\text{jitter}
]

Ship the best A/B winner, not the most complicated bot.

## Final finals objective

Qualifier bot:

[
\boxed{\text{survive and stay above cutoff}}
]

Finals bot:

[
\boxed{\text{scoop more tables without punting big-stack leads}}
]

Your finals build should be **less scared of pressure**, but **more cautious with non-nut hands in huge river pots**. That is the balance.
